T2.append(dict(id="mark-forum-buses", title="A forum post about free buses for under-18s", level="L2", missionId="w-transport-4", samples=[
 sample("e2", """Hi everyone,

I think buses should be free for under-18s, at least on journeys to school and college. Many families in our area already struggle with the cost of living and a weekly bus pass can cost more than the weekly food shop for a teenager's lunches.

The main argument for free travel is fairness. Students who live in the countryside often have no choice but to take the bus, whereas those in town can walk. For example, my cousin spends £18 a week to get to college, which is money his family could have spent on other things. Free buses would also mean fewer parents driving children to school, which would reduce traffic and pollution outside the gates.

However, someone has to pay for it. If the council cuts other services to fund free travel, then the benefit might be cancelled out. A fair compromise would be free travel at peak times only.

What do other people think the council should do?""",
  6, 4,
  "A clear view, with developed reasons, a real example and a counter-argument. Paragraphs are used and the tone suits a forum. Vocabulary and sentences are generally appropriate, though some phrasing is repetitive.",
  "Punctuation is generally correct across commas, apostrophes and colons. There are a few small slips with commas and one awkward comparison, so it is generally accurate.",
  [("You supported your view with a specific example.", "my cousin spends £18 a week to get to college"), ("You recognised the other side and offered a compromise.", "A fair compromise would be free travel at peak times only")],
  ("Make the link between your reasons clearer with linking words such as \"furthermore\", \"consequently\" or \"in contrast\", and tighten the comparison in your first paragraph.", "For example: \"Consequently, many teenagers cannot afford to travel to college at all.\""),
  [("a weekly bus pass can cost more than the weekly food shop for a teenager's lunches", "a weekly bus pass can cost as much as a teenager's lunches for the week", "The comparison was muddled, so it did not say clearly what is being compared.")]),
 sample("e1", """I think that buses should be free for under 18s because its not fair that teenagers have to pay.

Lots of young people dont have much money. They have to pay for the bus to college and its expensive. My friend pays alot every week and she has to ask her mum for money. Also if the buses was free then more people would use them and there would be less cars.

But the council would have to pay for it and that might mean they cut other things. So I think it is a good idea but they need to find the money from somewhere.

what do you think""",
  3, 2,
  "There is a clear view and a few reasons, but they are not developed and the example is general. The counter-point is brief. The tone is suitable but sentence structures are simple.",
  "Several apostrophes, capital letters and question marks are missing, and there are some spelling and grammar slips. The meaning is mostly clear.",
  [("You gave a view in your first sentence.", "buses should be free for under 18s"), ("You thought about who would pay.", "the council would have to pay for it")],
  ("Pick one reason and develop it with a specific example, then link your points with a connective.", "For example: \"Furthermore, free buses would help students who live far from college.\""),
  [("because its not fair", "because it's not fair", "\"It's\" means \"it is\"."), ("dont have much money", "don't have much money", "A short form needs an apostrophe."), ("alot", "a lot", "\"A lot\" is two words."), ("if the buses was free then more people would use them", "if the buses were free, more people would use them", "\"Buses\" takes \"were\", and a comma follows the opening clause."), ("what do you think", "What do you think?", "A capital letter at the start and a question mark at the end.")]),
 sample("e3", """Hi everyone,

In my view, buses should be free for everyone under 18, because the benefits to families and to the town outweigh the cost.

The strongest argument is fairness. A weekly student pass costs £18 in our area, which is a heavy burden for families already managing rising bills. Consequently, some teenagers skip college courses that would require a longer journey, simply because they cannot afford the fares. Free travel would give every young person the same chance to learn, regardless of income.

Moreover, there are wider benefits. If more teenagers used the bus, fewer parents would drive them to school, which would ease congestion and improve the air outside the gates. Where similar schemes have been tried in other cities, more young people have started using public transport.

Admittedly, someone must pay: the council could be forced to cut other services. Nevertheless, a limited scheme covering journeys to school and college would be affordable, and the long-term gains would be worth it.

How would you fund it, if you were in charge?""",
  8, 6,
  "A confident, well structured post. The view is clear, the reasons are developed with specific evidence, and a counter-argument is fairly considered and answered. Paragraphs are linked with connectives, the tone suits a forum and the vocabulary is wide.",
  "Punctuation is varied and accurate, including colons and commas, spelling is secure and grammar is consistent.",
  [("You answered the opposing argument fairly, then explained why your view still stands.", "Nevertheless, a limited scheme covering journeys to school and college would be affordable"), ("You linked your points clearly, so the argument builds.", "Consequently, some teenagers skip college courses")],
  ("To lift it further, back up your evidence by naming one place or source.", "For example: \"According to a council report, the number of young bus users rose by 12% after the scheme began.\""),
  []),
]))

T2.append(dict(id="mark-news-village", title="A newsletter item about a village", level="L2", missionId="w-geography-4", samples=[
 sample("f2", """Welcome to our village

Our village is a friendly place to live, and there is plenty to do whatever your age.

The village hall hosts a coffee morning every Thursday, which is a good way for new residents to meet people. For younger people, there is a football pitch on the green and a youth group on Friday evenings. Walkers will enjoy the footpath along the river, which leads to the old mill and takes about an hour. Every July the summer fair raises money for the church roof and the whole village comes along.

However, there are things we could improve. The bus only runs twice a day, so people without cars find it hard to get to the town. Broadband is also slow in some streets. We would like to see a regular bus and faster internet, and we should ask the parish council about it.

If you would like to help, come to the next meeting in the hall. New ideas are always welcome!""",
  6, 4,
  "Both bullet points are covered, with several relevant details and a clear idea of who each activity suits. The tone is friendly and positive, the format is acceptable and paragraphs are used. Language is clear but not varied.",
  "Generally correct punctuation and spelling. A few comma slips and one run-on sentence, but errors do not affect the meaning.",
  [("You matched each activity with the people it suits.", "For younger people, there is a football pitch on the green"), ("You invited readers to take part.", "If you would like to help, come to the next meeting in the hall")],
  ("Make the second paragraph more persuasive by explaining the benefit of each improvement.", "For example: \"A regular bus would let teenagers reach college and part-time jobs without needing a lift.\""),
  [("We would like to see a regular bus and faster internet, and we should ask the parish council about it.", "We would like to see a regular bus and faster internet, so we should ask the parish council about it.", "\"And\" joins ideas of equal weight, whereas \"so\" shows the reason for asking.")]),
 sample("f1", """our village is a good place to live. There is a shop and a hall and a park. The hall has events. Its good for old people and the park is good for kids.

The village could be better if the bus came more. and there was more things to do. Come to the meeting if you want to help""",
  3, 2,
  "Some relevant ideas are given for both bullets, but they are only listed, with little detail. The format has no heading and the sentences are short and simple.",
  "There are several errors with capital letters, full stops and apostrophes, and a grammar slip, but the writing can be understood.",
  [("You mentioned several places in the village.", "There is a shop and a hall and a park"), ("You ended with an invitation.", "Come to the meeting if you want to help")],
  ("Give a detail about each place, for example when it is open or what happens there, and add a heading.", "For example: \"The hall hosts a lunch club every Wednesday for older residents.\""),
  [("our village", "Our village", "Start the first word with a capital letter."), ("Its good for old people", "It's good for older people", "\"It's\" means \"it is\"."), ("if the bus came more. and there was more things", "if the bus came more often and there were more things", "A sentence should not start with \"and\" after a full stop, and \"things\" takes \"were\"."), ("if you want to help", "if you want to help.", "End the sentence with a full stop.")]),
 sample("f3", """Welcome to Elmstead: a village for everyone

Whether you have lived here for fifty years or fifty days, Elmstead has something for you.

The village hall is the heart of our community. Every Thursday, the coffee morning brings neighbours together, and the Friday youth club gives teenagers a place to meet, play table tennis and make music. Walkers will love the riverside path, which winds for three miles to the old mill, while families can enjoy the green, where the summer fair raises hundreds of pounds for the church roof each July.

Nevertheless, a good village can always improve. At present, the bus runs only twice a day, which leaves anyone without a car stranded. A regular service would help teenagers to reach college and older residents to attend appointments in town. Faster broadband would also benefit people who work from home.

We would love you to join us. Come along to the parish meeting on the first Monday of each month, or speak to any member of the committee. Together, we can make Elmstead even better.""",
  8, 6,
  "Strong and engaging, with an inviting heading and a welcoming tone that fits a newsletter. Both bullets are covered in detail, benefits are explained for particular groups, and the ending makes a clear call to action. Language and sentence structures are varied.",
  "Punctuation is consistently correct, including colons and commas, with secure spelling and grammar.",
  [("You explained who each improvement would help.", "A regular service would help teenagers to reach college and older residents to attend appointments in town"), ("Your heading and opening welcome every reader, new or old.", "Whether you have lived here for fifty years or fifty days")],
  ("To go further, include one short quotation or a real-sounding detail from a villager, which would add a personal voice.", "For example: \"As one resident says, 'It's the only place where everyone says hello.'\""),
  []),
]))
